\begin{abstract}
Science maps use relationships between papers to describe how research is organised. When these relationships come from text embeddings, a model turns each paper into a list of numbers. Would another model give the same picture? I compare ten models using the same collection of 500,000 OpenAlex records, covering 26 research fields and 2000--2024. I examine the overall arrangement, the papers placed closest to each other, and the spread of papers within fields. The models share a broad arrangement, but in searches within the same field and period, two models retain about eight of the same 25 closest papers on average. Using only the title instead of the title and abstract changes these neighbours about as much as changing models. In a later exploratory comparison, models also disagree about which of two fields has more widely spread paper representations. These opposite results can persist when different articles are selected, although many differences are small and processing choices matter. Repeating a result with other articles therefore does not ensure that another model supports it. The findings identify dependence on representation; they do not establish which model best describes the research itself.
\end{abstract}
\textbf{Keywords:} science mapping; document embeddings; robustness; neighbourhood agreement; geometric comparisons.

